{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "```python\ndef closest_integer(value):\n    '''\n    Returns the closest integer to the given string representation of a number.\n    Numbers equidistant from two integers are rounded away from zero.\n    '''\n    num = float(value)\n\n    if num >= 0:\n        floor_val = int(num)\n        ceil_val = floor_val + 1\n        if num - floor_val >= 0.5:\n            return ceil_val\n        else:\n            return floor_val\n    else:\n        ceil_val = int(num)\n        floor_val = ceil_val - 1\n        if abs(num - floor_val) <= abs(num - ceil_val):\n            return floor_val\n        else:\n            return ceil_val\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates a list representing a pile of n levels of stones.\n    The first level has n stones, and each subsequent level has 2 more stones.\n    \"\"\"\n    pile = []\n    current_stones = n\n    for _ in range(n):\n        pile.append(current_stones)\n        current_stones += 2\n    return pile"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/104", "solution": "```python\ndef unique_digits(x):\n    \"\"\"Given a list of positive integers x, return a sorted list of all \n    elements that hasn't any even digit.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for num in x:\n        has_even_digit = False\n        temp_num = num\n        while temp_num > 0:\n            digit = temp_num % 10\n            if digit % 2 == 0:\n                has_even_digit = True\n                break\n            temp_num //= 10\n        \n        if not has_even_digit:\n            result.append(num)\n            \n    result.sort()\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/106", "solution": "```python\ndef f(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes a list of size n where each element is either the factorial of its index\n    (if even) or the sum of numbers up to its index (if odd), starting from index 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # If i is even, calculate factorial\n            factorial_val = 1\n            for j in range(1, i + 1):\n                factorial_val *= j\n            result.append(factorial_val)\n        else:  # If i is odd, calculate sum from 1 to i\n            sum_val = 0\n            for j in range(1, i + 1):\n                sum_val += j\n            result.append(sum_val)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/107", "solution": "```python\ndef even_odd_palindrome(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts even and odd integer palindromes up to n (inclusive).\n    It iterates from 1 to n, checks if each number is a palindrome, and tallies counts based on parity.\n    \"\"\"\n    even_count = 0\n    odd_count = 0\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        s = str(i)\n        if s == s[::-1]:\n            if i % 2 == 0:\n                even_count += 1\n            else:\n                odd_count += 1\n    return (even_count, odd_count)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/109", "solution": "```python\ndef move_one_ball(arr):\n    \"\"\"We have an array 'arr' of N integers arr[1], arr[2], ..., arr[N].The\n    numbers in the array will be randomly ordered. Your task is to determine if\n    it is possible to get an array sorted in non-decreasing order by performing \n    the following operation on the given array:\n        You are allowed to perform right shift operation any number of times.\n    \n    One right shift operation means shifting all elements of the array by one\n    position in the right direction. The last element of the array will be moved to\n    the starting position in the array i.e. 0th index. \n\n    If it is possible to obtain the sorted array by performing the above operation\n    then return True else return False.\n    If the given array is empty then return True.\n\n    Note: The given list is guaranteed to have unique elements.\n\n    For Example:\n    \n    move_one_ball([3, 4, 5, 1, 2])==>True\n    Explanation: By performin 2 right shift operations, non-decreasing order can\n                 be achieved for the given array.\n    move_one_ball([3, 5, 4, 1, 2])==>False\n    Explanation:It is not possible to get non-decreasing order for the given\n                array by performing any number of right shift operations.\n                \n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return True\n\n    sorted_arr = sorted(arr)\n    current_arr = list(arr) \n\n    for _ in range(n):\n        if current_arr == sorted_arr:\n            return True\n        \n        last_element = current_arr.pop()\n        current_arr.insert(0, last_element)\n            \n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "def split_words(txt):\n    '''\n    Given a string of words, return a list of words split on whitespace, if no whitespaces exists in the text you\n    should split on commas ',' if no commas exists you should return the number of lower-case letters with odd order in the\n    alphabet, ord('a') = 0, ord('b') = 1, ... ord('z') = 25\n    '''\n    if ' ' in txt:\n        return txt.split(' ')\n    elif ',' in txt:\n        return txt.split(',')\n    else:\n        odd_order_count = 0\n        for char in txt:\n            if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n                if (ord(char) - ord('a')) % 2 != 0:\n                    odd_order_count += 1\n        return odd_order_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "def tri(n):\n    \"\"\"Generates the first n+1 terms of the Tribonacci sequence as defined.\n    The sequence values are determined by rules for even, odd, and a base case.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    sequence = []\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        if i == 1:\n            # Explicit base case: tri(1) = 3\n            current_tri_value = 3\n        elif i % 2 == 0:\n            # Rule for even numbers: tri(n) = 1 + n / 2 (using integer division)\n            current_tri_value = 1 + i // 2\n        else: # i is odd and i != 1\n            # Rule for odd numbers: tri(n) = tri(n - 1) + tri(n - 2) + tri(n + 1)\n            # tri(n+1) is for an even number, so its value is calculated using the even rule\n            prev1 = sequence[i - 1]\n            prev2 = sequence[i - 2]\n            next_even_term_value = 1 + (i + 1) // 2\n            current_tri_value = prev1 + prev2 + next_even_term_value\n        sequence.append(current_tri_value)\n    return sequence"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "def is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"Checks if the input string contains a subsequence representing nested square brackets.\n    Returns True if a '[[...]]' pattern is found as a subsequence, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # This function finds if four brackets '[', '[', ']', ']' exist in sequential order as a subsequence.\n\n    first_open_idx = -1\n    for i, char in enumerate(string):\n        if char == '[':\n            first_open_idx = i\n            break\n    if first_open_idx == -1:\n        return False\n\n    second_open_idx = -1\n    for i in range(first_open_idx + 1, len(string)):\n        if string[i] == '[':\n            second_open_idx = i\n            break\n    if second_open_idx == -1:\n        return False\n\n    first_close_idx = -1\n    for i in range(second_open_idx + 1, len(string)):\n        if string[i] == ']':\n            first_close_idx = i\n            break\n    if first_close_idx == -1:\n        return False\n\n    second_close_idx = -1\n    for i in range(first_close_idx + 1, len(string)):\n        if string[i] == ']':\n            second_close_idx = i\n            break\n    if second_close_idx == -1:\n        return False\n\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "```python\ndef digits(n):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer n, return the product of its odd digits.\n    Returns 0 if all digits are even.\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1\n    for digit in str(n):\n        if int(digit) % 2 == 1:\n            product *= int(digit)\n    return product if product != 1 else 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "```python\ndef sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"You are given a list of numbers.\n    You need to return the sum of squared numbers in the given list,\n    round each element in the list to the upper int(Ceiling) first.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for x in lst:\n        # Custom ceiling implementation\n        if x == int(x):\n            ceiled_x = int(x)\n        elif x > 0:\n            ceiled_x = int(x) + 1\n        else:  # x < 0 and not an integer\n            ceiled_x = int(x)\n        \n        total_sum += ceiled_x * ceiled_x\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\n\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the mean absolute deviation (MAD) of a list of numbers.\n    If the input list is empty, it returns 0.0.\"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0.0\n    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n    return sum(abs(x - mean) for x in numbers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "```python\ndef pairs_sum_to_zero(l):\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    It returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    seen = set()\n    for num in l:\n        if -num in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(num)\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/55", "solution": "```python\ndef fib(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Return n-th Fibonacci number.\n    Calculates the number iteratively, handling base cases for n=0 and n=1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return n\n    \n    a, b = 0, 1\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, a + b\n    return b\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/56", "solution": "```python\ndef correct_bracketing(brackets: str):\n    \"\"\" brackets is a string of \"<\" and \">\".\n    return True if every opening bracket has a corresponding closing bracket.\n    \"\"\"\n    balance = 0\n    for char in brackets:\n        if char == '<':\n            balance += 1\n        elif char == '>':\n            balance -= 1\n        if balance < 0:\n            return False\n    return balance == 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/60", "solution": "```python\ndef sum_to_n(n: int):\n    \"\"\"Sums numbers from 1 to n using the arithmetic series formula.\n    This method provides an efficient O(1) solution.\n    \"\"\"\n    return n * (n + 1) // 2\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/101", "solution": "def words_string(s):\n    \"\"\"\n    Splits a string by commas or spaces into an array of words.\n    Handles multiple delimiters and returns a list of cleaned words.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Replace all commas with spaces to unify delimiters, then split by whitespace.\n    return s.replace(\",\", \" \").split()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/103", "solution": "def rounded_avg(n, m):\n    \"\"\"Computes the average of integers from n to m, rounds it, and converts to binary.\n    Returns -1 if n is greater than m.\"\"\"\n    if n > m:\n        return -1\n    \n    # Calculate the average of the arithmetic series (n, n+1, ..., m).\n    average = (n + m) / 2\n    \n    # Round the average to the nearest integer.\n    rounded_value = round(average)\n    \n    # Convert the rounded integer to its binary string representation.\n    return bin(rounded_value)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/106", "solution": "def f(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Implements the function f that generates a list based on index parity.\n    For even indices, it calculates factorial; for odd, it calculates sum.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # i is even, calculate factorial of i\n            current_value = 1\n            for k in range(1, i + 1):\n                current_value *= k\n            result.append(current_value)\n        else:  # i is odd, calculate sum of numbers from 1 to i\n            current_value = 0\n            for k in range(1, i + 1):\n                current_value += k\n            result.append(current_value)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/111", "solution": "```python\ndef histogram(test):\n    \"\"\"Counts character frequencies in a space-separated string, returning those with maximum repetition.\n    If multiple characters share the highest count, all are included in the result.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not test:\n        return {}\n\n    counts = {}\n    letters = test.split(' ')\n\n    for letter in letters:\n        # Only process non-empty strings, assuming they represent individual letters.\n        if letter:\n            counts[letter] = counts.get(letter, 0) + 1\n\n    if not counts:\n        return {}\n\n    max_count = 0\n    for count in counts.values():\n        if count > max_count:\n            max_count = count\n\n    result = {}\n    for letter, count in counts.items():\n        if count == max_count:\n            result[letter] = count\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/115", "solution": "import math\n\ndef max_fill(grid, capacity):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the minimum number of times buckets need to be lowered\n    to empty all wells in the grid.\n    Each 1 in a row is a unit of water, and buckets have a fixed capacity.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_lowers = 0\n    for well in grid:\n        water_in_well = sum(well)\n        if water_in_well > 0:\n            lowers_for_well = math.ceil(water_in_well / capacity)\n            total_lowers += lowers_for_well\n    return total_lowers"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of n-digit numbers that start or end with 1.\n    This uses the principle of inclusion-exclusion.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    else:\n        # Numbers that start with 1: 1 followed by (n-1) digits (10^(n-1) possibilities)\n        # Numbers that end with 1: (n-1) digits followed by 1. The first of these (n-1) digits cannot be 0.\n        # There are 9 choices for the first digit and 10 choices for the next (n-2) digits (9 * 10^(n-2)).\n        # Numbers that start and end with 1: 1 followed by (n-2) digits followed by 1 (10^(n-2) possibilities).\n        # Total = (Starts with 1) + (Ends with 1) - (Starts and Ends with 1)\n        # Total = 10**(n - 1) + (9 * (10**(n - 2))) - (10**(n - 2))\n        # Total = 10**(n - 1) + 8 * (10**(n - 2))\n        return 10**(n - 1) + 8 * (10**(n - 2))\n```"}
